<section class="section advanced" id="architecture">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div class="eyebrow">Advanced learning module</div>
      <h2>Opportunity Architecture: Build Useful, Verifiable Systems</h2>
      <p>
        Learning can become more valuable when you apply it to real problems.
        This module teaches you to identify operational friction, design
        practical solutions, and evaluate whether your work is useful.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="advanced-intro">
      <h3>The central principle</h3>
      <p>
        Do not begin with a tool simply because it is interesting to build.
        Begin with a problem that creates unnecessary work, delays, confusion,
        or repeated errors. Then design a solution that can be tested.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3 class="advanced-subtitle">1. Structural Foundations of High-Leverage Assets</h3>
    <div class="advanced-grid">
      <article class="advanced-card">
        <div class="card-icon">⚙️</div>
        <h3>Independent Execution</h3>
        <p>
          Create tools, documents, and processes that can perform a defined
          task without requiring constant intervention from their creator.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Evidence:</strong> A working demonstration, documented process, or repeatable output.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="advanced-card">
        <div class="card-icon">🏗️</div>
        <h3>Durable Architecture</h3>
        <p>
          Use clear structures, reusable components, and maintainable
          instructions so that the solution can be understood and improved.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Evidence:</strong> Organized code, instructions, reusable templates, and clear inputs.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="advanced-card">
        <div class="card-icon">👁️</div>
        <h3>Immediate Verifiability</h3>
        <p>
          Make it easy for another person to understand what the asset does
          and test its usefulness without a lengthy explanation.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Evidence:</strong> A concise demonstration, before-and-after comparison, or test result.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <h3 class="advanced-subtitle">2. Identifying High-Value Friction</h3>
    <p style="color:#E8DED1">
      Look for repeated tasks, avoidable handoffs, information gaps, and
      processes that depend too heavily on memory or manual repetition.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Friction Type</th>
            <th>What You Observe</th>
            <th>Possible Solution</th>
            <th>Risk Consideration</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Data Bridge Lag</strong></td>
            <td>People repeatedly copy information between systems.</td>
            <td>A parser, read-only transformation, or approved integration.</td>
            <td>Keep data access limited and protect sensitive information.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Handoff Friction</strong></td>
            <td>Tasks lose context when responsibility changes.</td>
            <td>A status tracker, handoff template, or structured record.</td>
            <td>Standardize the information people need to pass along.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Verification Overhead</strong></td>
            <td>Managers repeatedly check the same work manually.</td>
            <td>Sanity checks, status flags, or an audit dashboard.</td>
            <td>Define acceptance criteria and preserve human review.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Knowledge Lock</strong></td>
            <td>Important steps exist only in someone's memory.</td>
            <td>Interactive guides, reference sheets, and documented procedures.</td>
            <td>Validate instructions with the people who perform the work.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="advanced-subtitle">3. Deployment and Delivery Mechanics</h3>
    <div class="stage-list">
      <article class="stage">
        <h3>Stage 1: Identify the Root Cause</h3>
        <p>
          Trace the process backward from the failure point. Ask where
          information becomes delayed, incomplete, duplicated, or difficult
          to verify.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Deliverable:</strong> A written problem statement and process map.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="stage">
        <h3>Stage 2: Build a Deterministic Asset</h3>
        <p>
          Define the inputs, operations, and expected outputs. Keep the
          initial version simple and use technologies appropriate to the task.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Deliverable:</strong> A working prototype with test cases.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="stage">
        <h3>Stage 3: Deliver With Risk Controls</h3>
        <p>
          Keep the solution optional and non-invasive. Where appropriate,
          test alongside the existing process before suggesting adoption.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Deliverable:</strong> A comparison of results and limitations.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="pipeline">
      <h3 style="margin-bottom:20px">A simple system architecture</h3>
      <div class="pipeline-flow">
        <div class="pipeline-item">Trigger Event</div>
        <div class="pipeline-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="pipeline-item">Transform Unit</div>
        <div class="pipeline-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="pipeline-item">Output Target</div>
      </div>
      <p style="color:#DCEAF1;margin-top:18px">
        Define what starts the process, what happens to the information,
        and what successful output looks like.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3 class="advanced-subtitle">4. Illustrative Case Study Patterns</h3>
    <p style="color:#E8DED1">
      The following are hypothetical examples for practicing the method,
      not verified client outcomes or performance claims.
    </p>

    <div class="example-grid">
      <article class="example">
        <h3>Example A: Administrative Bottleneck</h3>
        <p><strong>Observed issue:</strong> A front desk employee enters the same information into multiple places.</p>
        <p><strong>Possible approach:</strong> Map the workflow, identify duplicate entry, and prototype a permitted data-transfer or form-validation aid.</p>
        <p><strong>Proof to collect:</strong> Time spent before and after, error counts, and user feedback.</p>
        <p><strong>Important:</strong> Obtain permission before accessing or changing workplace systems or customer information.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="example">
        <h3>Example B: Independent Service Pitch</h3>
        <p><strong>Observed issue:</strong> A business website has a confusing mobile menu or broken image display.</p>
        <p><strong>Possible approach:</strong> Create a demonstration using a permitted copy or local prototype and document the issue.</p>
        <p><strong>Proof to collect:</strong> Screenshots, a short screen recording, and a working demonstration.</p>
        <p><strong>Important:</strong> Do not change a live website without authorization.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <h3 class="advanced-subtitle">5. Communication Template: Presenting a Solution</h3>
    <div class="template">
      <p><strong>Subject:</strong> A possible way to simplify [specific process]</p>
      <p>Hello [Name],</p>
      <p>
        I noticed that [describe the specific repeated task, delay, or
        problem]. I have been exploring a way to make this process easier
        through [briefly describe the proposed solution].
      </p>
      <p>
        I prepared a small demonstration showing how the approach could work.
        It is intended to complement your existing process rather than require
        an immediate change.
      </p>
      <p>
        If useful, I would be glad to show you the prototype and discuss
        whether it addresses the problem.
      </p>
      <p>Thank you,<br>[Your Name]</p>
      <button class="btn btn-primary" type="button" id="copyTemplate">Copy Communication Template</button>
    </div>

    <h3 class="advanced-subtitle">6. Asset Evaluation and Risk Diagnostic</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Evaluation Area</th>
            <th>Question to Ask</th>
            <th>Warning Sign</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Access Barrier</strong></td>
            <td>Can someone test the solution with minimal steps?</td>
            <td>It requires unnecessary installation, account creation, or permissions.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Cognitive Overhead</strong></td>
            <td>Can a person understand the benefit quickly?</td>
            <td>The explanation relies on jargon or unclear claims.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Dependency Risk</strong></td>
            <td>Can the solution operate reliably with its stated dependencies?</td>
            <td>It relies on fragile or undocumented external components.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Operational Fit</strong></td>
            <td>Does it fit the existing process and user habits?</td>
            <td>It forces people into a new platform or unnecessary workflow.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="advanced-subtitle">7. Actionable System Audit Checklist</h3>
    <p style="color:#E8DED1">
      Check each item after evaluating a project or prototype. Your progress
